1
00:00:01,669 --> 00:00:04,421
Hey, you guys, what are you
doing tomorrow night?

2
00:00:04,463 --> 00:00:05,589
Well, let
me see.

3
00:00:05,631 --> 00:00:07,007
I believe I'm...

4
00:00:07,048 --> 00:00:09,718
yes, falling asleep
in front of the TV.

5
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:12,721
My agent hooked me up
with six tickets

6
00:00:12,763 --> 00:00:14,055
to a great play.

7
00:00:14,097 --> 00:00:15,891
I could fall asleep at a play.

8
00:00:16,892 --> 00:00:18,226
What is it?

9
00:00:18,268 --> 00:00:19,854
It's a one-woman play called,

10
00:00:19,895 --> 00:00:21,396
Why Don't You Like Me?

11
00:00:21,438 --> 00:00:23,482
A Bitter Woman's Journey
Through Life.

12
00:00:23,524 --> 00:00:25,275
That sounds interesting.

13
00:00:25,317 --> 00:00:26,861
That does sound interesting.

14
00:00:26,902 --> 00:00:28,988
I mean, to listen to a woman
complain for two hours.

15
00:00:29,029 --> 00:00:30,364
I don't think it gets bett...

16
00:00:30,405 --> 00:00:31,406
( snoring )

17
00:00:33,492 --> 00:00:34,827
I know, I know.

18
00:00:34,869 --> 00:00:36,453
We can drive, we can vote,
we can work.

19
00:00:36,495 --> 00:00:37,997
What more do these broads want?

20
00:00:39,581 --> 00:00:42,250
Well, you guys are going
to have a great time, I promise.

21
00:00:42,292 --> 00:00:44,169
What? How
come you don't
have to go?

22
00:00:44,211 --> 00:00:45,880
I wish I could,
but I just found out

23
00:00:45,921 --> 00:00:48,465
that I have to be at work
really early the next day.

24
00:00:48,507 --> 00:00:49,800
So I can't go.

25
00:00:49,842 --> 00:00:51,050
But, you know, take
the extra ticket

26
00:00:51,092 --> 00:00:52,386
and invite whoever you want.

27
00:00:52,427 --> 00:00:55,514
Ooh, let's see, who do I hate?

28
00:00:57,474 --> 00:00:59,560
Oh, sorry.

29
00:00:59,601 --> 00:01:01,812
Oh, sorry.

30
00:01:01,854 --> 00:01:03,355
Hey, up we go.

31
00:01:03,397 --> 00:01:04,523
All right.

32
00:01:09,444 --> 00:01:11,279
Oh.

33
00:01:14,408 --> 00:01:15,910
Can I ask you
a question?

34
00:01:15,951 --> 00:01:17,578
Yeah.

35
00:01:17,619 --> 00:01:19,663
Do you think
it's possible

36
00:01:19,705 --> 00:01:22,123
for two friends
to fool around

37
00:01:22,165 --> 00:01:24,626
and not have it be
a big deal?

38
00:01:24,668 --> 00:01:26,754
No, I don't think
that ever works.

39
00:01:26,795 --> 00:01:28,547
Why?

40
00:01:28,589 --> 00:01:29,673
No reason.

41
00:01:29,715 --> 00:01:30,674
No, no. Rachel?

42
00:01:30,716 --> 00:01:31,842
Yeah?

43
00:01:31,884 --> 00:01:33,427
Who do you want
to fool around with?

44
00:01:33,468 --> 00:01:34,803
Nobody. Forget it.

45
00:01:38,057 --> 00:01:39,934
( whispers ):
Maybe.

46
00:01:39,975 --> 00:01:41,936
You can't.

47
00:01:41,977 --> 00:01:43,186
Why?

48
00:01:43,228 --> 00:01:46,774
( whispering )

49
00:01:46,815 --> 00:01:50,069
Seriously, I did not understand
a word you just said.

50
00:01:50,110 --> 00:01:52,279
In the hall.

51
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:54,364
All right.

52
00:01:55,908 --> 00:01:58,118
You want to fool around
with Joey?

53
00:01:58,159 --> 00:01:59,494
Yeah. You know what?

54
00:01:59,536 --> 00:02:01,080
Ever since
I had that dream about him,

55
00:02:01,121 --> 00:02:02,581
I can't get it
out of my head.

56
00:02:02,623 --> 00:02:04,041
I mean, what's the big deal?

57
00:02:04,083 --> 00:02:05,375
People do it all time.

58
00:02:05,417 --> 00:02:07,335
Who? Who do you know
that are friends

59
00:02:07,377 --> 00:02:08,670
that just fool around?

60
00:02:08,712 --> 00:02:11,131
Okay, off the top of my head...

61
00:02:11,172 --> 00:02:12,424
Don and Janet.

62
00:02:12,466 --> 00:02:13,634
Who are they?

63
00:02:13,675 --> 00:02:15,302
I, uh, I know them
from work.

64
00:02:15,343 --> 00:02:16,678
Both of them?

65
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:18,221
No, one of them.

66
00:02:18,263 --> 00:02:19,138
Which one?

67
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:20,307
I don't know.

68
00:02:20,348 --> 00:02:23,310
What were the names I just said?

69
00:02:23,351 --> 00:02:26,229
Rachel, things could get
incredibly complicated.

70
00:02:26,271 --> 00:02:28,023
All right, all right, all right.
You're right.

71
00:02:28,065 --> 00:02:29,232
I won't do anything
with Joey.

72
00:02:29,274 --> 00:02:30,692
I just thought it would...

73
00:02:30,734 --> 00:02:32,653
Okay, that would be
two cups of tarragon,

74
00:02:32,694 --> 00:02:34,905
one pound of baking soda
and one red onion?

75
00:02:34,947 --> 00:02:38,033
What the hell are you cooking?

76
00:02:44,748 --> 00:02:49,377
♪ So no one told you
life was gonna be this way ♪

77
00:02:49,419 --> 00:02:52,506
♪ Your job's a joke,
you're broke ♪

78
00:02:52,547 --> 00:02:55,009
♪ Your love life's D.O.A. ♪

79
00:02:55,050 --> 00:02:58,971
♪ It's like you're always
stuck in second gear ♪

80
00:02:59,013 --> 00:03:01,974
♪ When it hasn't been
your day, your week ♪

81
00:03:02,016 --> 00:03:04,601
♪ Your month,
or even your year ♪

82
00:03:04,643 --> 00:03:08,271
♪ But I'll be there for you ♪

83
00:03:08,313 --> 00:03:10,858
♪ When the rain starts to pour ♪

84
00:03:10,899 --> 00:03:13,276
♪ I'll be there for you ♪

85
00:03:13,318 --> 00:03:15,821
♪ Like I've been there before ♪

86
00:03:15,863 --> 00:03:18,991
♪ I'll be there for you ♪

87
00:03:19,033 --> 00:03:22,201
♪ 'Cause you're there
for me, too. ♪

88
00:03:30,460 --> 00:03:31,753
Hey.

89
00:03:31,795 --> 00:03:32,838
Hey.

90
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:34,131
You guys won't believe

91
00:03:34,173 --> 00:03:35,590
what I have to do
for work today.

92
00:03:35,632 --> 00:03:37,801
Yes, but Ross,
you chose a career

93
00:03:37,843 --> 00:03:39,469
of talking
about dinosaurs.

94
00:03:39,511 --> 00:03:41,346
There are these
two professors

95
00:03:41,388 --> 00:03:44,058
who are joining
my department,

96
00:03:44,099 --> 00:03:48,311
and I have to meet them here
and show them around campus.

97
00:03:48,353 --> 00:03:50,064
What's so bad
about that?

98
00:03:50,105 --> 00:03:53,274
Well, I just know they're going
to be couple of old windbags

99
00:03:53,316 --> 00:03:56,195
wearing tweed jackets
with suede elbow patches.

100
00:03:56,236 --> 00:03:58,279
Ross...

101
00:03:58,321 --> 00:04:01,158
These aren't suede.

102
00:04:04,119 --> 00:04:05,620
Excuse me.

103
00:04:05,662 --> 00:04:07,581
I'm looking
for someone.

104
00:04:07,622 --> 00:04:09,416
You don't by
any chance

105
00:04:09,457 --> 00:04:11,085
know a Ross Geller?

106
00:04:11,126 --> 00:04:13,128
No.

107
00:04:14,838 --> 00:04:16,339
Hi.

108
00:04:16,381 --> 00:04:18,675
Hi. I'm Ross Geller.

109
00:04:18,717 --> 00:04:20,094
Oh, hi.

110
00:04:20,135 --> 00:04:22,721
I'm Professor Wheeler.

111
00:04:22,763 --> 00:04:24,598
Oh... oh, that's...

112
00:04:24,639 --> 00:04:26,600
that's, that's nice.

113
00:04:26,641 --> 00:04:27,976
It's good to meet you.

114
00:04:28,018 --> 00:04:30,353
Thank you so much
for taking the time out

115
00:04:30,395 --> 00:04:31,438
to show me around.

116
00:04:31,479 --> 00:04:33,023
Oh, no, it's no big deal.

117
00:04:33,065 --> 00:04:35,817
I mean, if I weren't doing this,
I'd just, you know,

118
00:04:35,859 --> 00:04:37,360
be at the gym working out.

119
00:04:38,904 --> 00:04:40,488
Is he going
to introduce us?

120
00:04:40,530 --> 00:04:43,200
I think we're just
blurry shapes to him now.

121
00:04:44,576 --> 00:04:46,745
By the way, I really
enjoyed your paper

122
00:04:46,787 --> 00:04:49,372
on the connection between
geographic isolation

123
00:04:49,414 --> 00:04:50,665
and rapid mutagenesis.

124
00:04:50,707 --> 00:04:52,333
Oh...

125
00:04:52,375 --> 00:04:54,795
I wrote that in one night.

126
00:04:54,836 --> 00:04:58,966
20 bucks says they're
married within the month.

127
00:04:59,007 --> 00:05:02,261
We should probably
get going.

128
00:05:02,301 --> 00:05:05,222
Um, you know, we've got
a lot of ground to cover.

129
00:05:05,264 --> 00:05:06,640
Oh, isn't there
another professor

130
00:05:06,681 --> 00:05:08,058
that's supposed
to come with us?

131
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:11,145
I don't think so.

132
00:05:11,186 --> 00:05:14,022
I'm pretty sure a Professor
Spafford from Cornell?

133
00:05:14,064 --> 00:05:15,565
Well, he's
obviously late

134
00:05:15,607 --> 00:05:19,236
and the rule in my class is,
if you can't come on time,

135
00:05:19,278 --> 00:05:20,821
then don't come at all.

136
00:05:20,862 --> 00:05:24,283
An option that many
of my students use.

137
00:05:24,324 --> 00:05:26,076
Shall we?

138
00:05:26,118 --> 00:05:27,661
You don't think
we should wait for him?

139
00:05:27,702 --> 00:05:29,121
Oh, you know what?
He's a big boy.

140
00:05:29,163 --> 00:05:30,413
I'm sure he'll find us,
okay?

141
00:05:30,455 --> 00:05:32,207
Professor Geller?

142
00:05:32,249 --> 00:05:33,667
Aw, damn it.

143
00:05:42,592 --> 00:05:43,844
( beep )

144
00:05:43,885 --> 00:05:45,344
WOMAN:
Hi, Joey. It's Jan Rogers.

145
00:05:45,386 --> 00:05:47,014
Can't wait for
your party tonight.

146
00:05:47,055 --> 00:05:49,057
Listen, I forgot your address.

147
00:05:49,099 --> 00:05:50,684
Can you give me a call?

148
00:05:50,725 --> 00:05:51,810
Thanks. Bye.

149
00:05:51,852 --> 00:05:53,020
( humming tune )

150
00:05:53,061 --> 00:05:54,062
Hey.

151
00:05:54,104 --> 00:05:55,063
Hey.

152
00:05:55,105 --> 00:05:56,439
What's happening?

153
00:05:56,481 --> 00:05:58,942
Yeah, it's a real shame
you can't make it

154
00:05:58,984 --> 00:06:00,861
to that one-woman
show tonight.

155
00:06:00,902 --> 00:06:04,239
Oh, I'd love to, but I got
to get up so early the next day.

156
00:06:04,281 --> 00:06:06,033
You know me,
work comes first.

157
00:06:06,074 --> 00:06:07,993
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

158
00:06:08,035 --> 00:06:10,037
Hi, Joey. It's Jan Rogers.

159
00:06:10,078 --> 00:06:11,538
Can't wait for
your party tonight...

160
00:06:14,708 --> 00:06:17,836
Stupid Jan Rogers!

161
00:06:17,878 --> 00:06:20,088
You are having
a party tonight?!

162
00:06:20,130 --> 00:06:21,548
Kind of having a thing

163
00:06:21,589 --> 00:06:23,382
for the Days of Our Lives
people.

164
00:06:23,424 --> 00:06:25,384
And you weren't
going to tell us?

165
00:06:25,426 --> 00:06:27,971
How did you think you were going
to get away with that?

166
00:06:28,013 --> 00:06:29,973
I do it every year.

167
00:06:31,516 --> 00:06:33,768
You do that
every year?!

168
00:06:33,810 --> 00:06:35,979
I didn't have
to tell you that.

169
00:06:36,021 --> 00:06:39,024
God, I'm stupider than
Jan Rogers.

170
00:06:39,066 --> 00:06:40,525
( gasps )

171
00:06:40,567 --> 00:06:42,110
That's why you got us
tickets to that play.

172
00:06:42,152 --> 00:06:43,237
To get rid of us.

173
00:06:43,278 --> 00:06:44,779
Yeah.

174
00:06:44,821 --> 00:06:46,740
And last year, is that why
you sent us to that

175
00:06:46,781 --> 00:06:48,075
Medieval Times restaurant?

176
00:06:48,116 --> 00:06:50,244
Yeah.

177
00:06:50,285 --> 00:06:51,953
And the year before that

178
00:06:51,995 --> 00:06:54,998
when you set up that nighttime
tour of that button factory?

179
00:06:55,040 --> 00:06:58,293
I can't believe you guys
went for that one.

180
00:06:58,335 --> 00:07:00,712
Joey, why wouldn't you invite us
to your parties?

181
00:07:00,754 --> 00:07:02,297
You're fine, okay?

182
00:07:02,339 --> 00:07:04,091
But everyone else acts
like an idiot

183
00:07:04,132 --> 00:07:05,217
around famous people.

184
00:07:05,259 --> 00:07:08,303
Well, then so just invite me.

185
00:07:08,345 --> 00:07:10,013
Please, I was trying to be nice.

186
00:07:10,055 --> 00:07:11,556
You're the worst one.

187
00:07:11,598 --> 00:07:14,433
Oh, Joey, come on, please,
please, let me come.

188
00:07:14,475 --> 00:07:16,270
I will behave, I promise.

189
00:07:16,311 --> 00:07:18,313
I will behave.
Please, please, please...

190
00:07:18,355 --> 00:07:19,940
Okay, okay, fine,
you can come.

191
00:07:19,981 --> 00:07:21,775
But don't tell
anybody else.

192
00:07:21,816 --> 00:07:23,735
It's up on the roof
at 8:00.

193
00:07:23,777 --> 00:07:26,238
A soap opera roof party?!

194
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:28,073
I'm going to a soap opera
roof party?!

195
00:07:28,115 --> 00:07:29,283
Oh, my God! Oh, my God!

196
00:07:30,992 --> 00:07:34,079
And it's out of my system.

197
00:07:39,667 --> 00:07:44,756
( speaking slowly ):
And then my wife
and I went on a cruise

198
00:07:44,798 --> 00:07:46,341
to the Galapagos.

199
00:07:46,383 --> 00:07:49,886
There was a seafood buffet
you wouldn't believe.

200
00:07:49,928 --> 00:07:51,888
There were clams...

201
00:07:51,930 --> 00:07:53,472
and mussels...

202
00:07:53,514 --> 00:07:55,309
and oysters...

203
00:07:55,350 --> 00:07:57,560
and cracked crab...

204
00:07:57,602 --> 00:07:59,854
and snow crab...

205
00:07:59,896 --> 00:08:02,190
and king crab.

206
00:08:02,232 --> 00:08:05,860
It's a pity
I'm allergic to shellfish.

207
00:08:07,028 --> 00:08:10,282
So where did you get
your undergraduate degree?

208
00:08:10,324 --> 00:08:11,950
And that's not all
I'm allergic to.

209
00:08:11,992 --> 00:08:14,828
Oh, it's not over.

210
00:08:14,869 --> 00:08:18,332
I'm also allergic
to peanuts and cashews

211
00:08:18,373 --> 00:08:20,834
and almonds and filberts...

212
00:08:20,875 --> 00:08:22,627
So, basically all nuts.

213
00:08:22,669 --> 00:08:25,088
Interestingly, no.

214
00:08:25,130 --> 00:08:26,965
Kind of playing it
fast and loose

215
00:08:27,007 --> 00:08:28,633
with the word "interesting."

216
00:08:31,303 --> 00:08:33,388
If you'll excuse me, I'm
going to use the restroom.

217
00:08:36,599 --> 00:08:37,934
Oh, my God!

218
00:08:37,976 --> 00:08:41,188
I've lost the will
to live.

219
00:08:41,229 --> 00:08:43,023
Let's ditch him.

220
00:08:43,064 --> 00:08:44,399
What?

221
00:08:44,441 --> 00:08:45,608
Come on, while
he's still in
the bathroom.

222
00:08:45,650 --> 00:08:46,609
I'm begging you.

223
00:08:46,651 --> 00:08:47,986
Okay, okay, fine.

224
00:08:48,028 --> 00:08:50,238
But I just have
one question for you.

225
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:54,368
( imitating Spafford ):
When we exit, should we
walk or run

226
00:08:54,409 --> 00:08:56,161
or prance, or stroll...?

227
00:08:56,203 --> 00:08:57,787
Stop it! Stop it!

228
00:08:57,829 --> 00:08:59,789
He talks slow, but he
might pee fast, okay? Let's go.

229
00:09:10,217 --> 00:09:13,136
Oh, hey, you guys,
this is Charlie.

230
00:09:13,178 --> 00:09:16,348
Charlie, this is Phoebe
and my sister Monica.

231
00:09:16,390 --> 00:09:17,765
Hi.

232
00:09:17,807 --> 00:09:19,934
Charlie's going
to be joining my department.

233
00:09:19,976 --> 00:09:21,895
Oh, you're a
paleontologist, too?

234
00:09:21,936 --> 00:09:25,399
Oh, okay, now, what do you
think of Renyard's new theory

235
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:28,735
of species variegation
in segmented arthropods?

236
00:09:30,237 --> 00:09:32,030
Well, I think he's
a little out there,

237
00:09:32,072 --> 00:09:33,948
but he does have
some interesting ideas.

238
00:09:33,990 --> 00:09:34,949
Uh-huh.

239
00:09:34,991 --> 00:09:35,950
( phone ringing )

240
00:09:35,992 --> 00:09:36,993
Oh, I'm sorry.

241
00:09:37,035 --> 00:09:38,995
I have to take this.
Excuse me.

242
00:09:39,037 --> 00:09:41,873
Renyard's theory
of species variegation?

243
00:09:41,915 --> 00:09:44,042
Yeah, I saw the article
on your coffee table

244
00:09:44,084 --> 00:09:46,127
and memorized the title
to freak you out.

245
00:09:49,047 --> 00:09:51,132
So, did you two have fun?

246
00:09:51,174 --> 00:09:53,676
Oh, my God,
she's-she's great.

247
00:09:53,718 --> 00:09:55,678
I mean, we have
so much in common,

248
00:09:55,720 --> 00:09:58,139
and she's just cool,
you know, and funny.

249
00:09:58,181 --> 00:10:00,850
I don't know if you've noticed,
but she's a... ♪ hottie. ♪

250
00:10:02,060 --> 00:10:04,104
♪ Hi. ♪

251
00:10:07,399 --> 00:10:09,443
Hey. Hi, you guys.

252
00:10:09,484 --> 00:10:12,070
Listen, you know what?

253
00:10:12,112 --> 00:10:13,780
I'm not feeling
really well,

254
00:10:13,821 --> 00:10:16,866
and I don't think I'm going
to get to go to the play.

255
00:10:16,908 --> 00:10:18,410
Really? What's wrong?

256
00:10:18,452 --> 00:10:19,702
I don't know.

257
00:10:19,744 --> 00:10:21,621
I think it's kind of serious.

258
00:10:21,662 --> 00:10:25,375
Um, you know, I was watching
this thing on TV this morning

259
00:10:25,417 --> 00:10:26,960
about Newcastle Disease,

260
00:10:27,001 --> 00:10:28,795
and I think I might have it.

261
00:10:28,836 --> 00:10:31,631
Newcastle Disease is
a secretion-borne virus

262
00:10:31,672 --> 00:10:34,634
that only affects chickens
and other poultry.

263
00:10:36,094 --> 00:10:38,555
Okay, who's this?

264
00:10:38,597 --> 00:10:40,974
I'm sorry. Rachel,
this is Charlie Wheeler.

265
00:10:41,015 --> 00:10:42,058
She's a colleague.

266
00:10:42,100 --> 00:10:43,226
Oh, hi.

267
00:10:43,268 --> 00:10:44,936
I would shake your hand,

268
00:10:44,978 --> 00:10:47,939
but I'm sure you don't want
to get my chicken disease.

269
00:10:47,981 --> 00:10:50,024
Rachel, can I see
you for a second?

270
00:10:50,066 --> 00:10:52,026
Sure.

271
00:10:56,615 --> 00:10:58,158
You're not sick.

272
00:10:58,199 --> 00:11:00,327
What? Yes, I am.

273
00:11:00,368 --> 00:11:02,912
Okay, then why are you...
all dressed up?

274
00:11:02,954 --> 00:11:06,416
Becau... when you're sick
you do whatever you can

275
00:11:06,458 --> 00:11:08,960
to make yourself
feel better.

276
00:11:09,961 --> 00:11:11,129
You just want to stay home

277
00:11:11,171 --> 00:11:12,631
so you can make a move on Joey.

278
00:11:12,671 --> 00:11:13,881
Oh, no, no.

279
00:11:13,923 --> 00:11:15,049
I heard you before.

280
00:11:15,091 --> 00:11:16,801
That is so not what this is.

281
00:11:16,843 --> 00:11:18,052
Okay, then what is this?

282
00:11:19,762 --> 00:11:21,306
Okay...

283
00:11:21,348 --> 00:11:24,058
Joey is having a secret
Days of Our Lives party

284
00:11:24,100 --> 00:11:26,853
up on the roof, and he sent
you guys to the play

285
00:11:26,894 --> 00:11:27,937
to get rid of you.

286
00:11:27,979 --> 00:11:29,439
What?!

287
00:11:30,649 --> 00:11:32,609
What-what's going on?

288
00:11:32,651 --> 00:11:35,945
Joey is having a secret
Days of Our Lives party
up on the roof.

289
00:11:35,987 --> 00:11:38,615
And he didn't want
you guys to know about it,

290
00:11:38,657 --> 00:11:40,575
but I came over here
to tell you.

291
00:11:40,617 --> 00:11:42,577
I thought you came
to say you were sick.

292
00:11:42,619 --> 00:11:44,663
All right, professor
or detective?

293
00:11:44,703 --> 00:11:46,747
Wait. Joey's having
a party

294
00:11:46,789 --> 00:11:49,376
and he wasn't going
to invite us?

295
00:11:49,417 --> 00:11:50,960
Yeah, he does it
every year.

296
00:11:51,002 --> 00:11:53,296
That's why he's sending
you to the play,

297
00:11:53,338 --> 00:11:56,007
that's why he sent us
to that Medieval Restaurant

298
00:11:56,049 --> 00:11:57,509
and to that button factory.

299
00:11:57,551 --> 00:11:59,969
And that horrible museum tour?

300
00:12:00,011 --> 00:12:02,096
No, I arranged that.

301
00:12:04,516 --> 00:12:06,142
( knocking )

302
00:12:06,184 --> 00:12:08,895
Hey, you guys, I'm turning in.
Have fun.

303
00:12:11,481 --> 00:12:14,025
We know about your party,
Joey.

304
00:12:14,067 --> 00:12:14,859
What party?

305
00:12:14,901 --> 00:12:16,110
The game's over.

306
00:12:16,152 --> 00:12:17,654
Take off your robe.

307
00:12:17,696 --> 00:12:21,074
Okay, I mean...

308
00:12:21,115 --> 00:12:22,909
No!
No!
No!

309
00:12:22,950 --> 00:12:23,993
Close it up!

310
00:12:24,035 --> 00:12:26,454
Close it!

311
00:12:27,788 --> 00:12:29,874
Nice to meet you.

312
00:12:39,676 --> 00:12:41,844
Hey, you made it.
All right.

313
00:12:41,886 --> 00:12:44,222
Hey, glad you could make it.
Thanks for coming.

314
00:12:44,264 --> 00:12:46,057
Oh, my God, Kyle Lowder.

315
00:12:46,099 --> 00:12:47,684
Hi.

316
00:12:47,726 --> 00:12:49,852
I love you!

317
00:12:49,894 --> 00:12:52,021
Hey, that's why
I didn't invite you.

318
00:12:52,063 --> 00:12:53,814
You have to calm down,
all right?

319
00:12:53,856 --> 00:12:56,025
Just... go get yourself
a drink or something.

320
00:12:56,067 --> 00:12:57,318
Oh, yeah, that's
what you want.

321
00:12:57,360 --> 00:12:59,028
My inhibitions lowered.

322
00:12:59,070 --> 00:13:00,821
Hey.
Oh, my God,

323
00:13:00,863 --> 00:13:02,949
can you believe
we're surrounded by all this?

324
00:13:02,990 --> 00:13:04,367
I can barely control myself.

325
00:13:04,409 --> 00:13:05,535
Monica, you might

326
00:13:05,577 --> 00:13:07,078
want to remember
that you're married.

327
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:08,747
Where is Chandler
anyway?

328
00:13:08,787 --> 00:13:11,499
Oh, my God, Chandler.

329
00:13:17,922 --> 00:13:20,675
Where the hell is everybody?

330
00:13:22,885 --> 00:13:25,722
Why don't you like me?

331
00:13:29,517 --> 00:13:32,937
Chapter One: My First Period.

332
00:13:37,191 --> 00:13:39,360
Hey, Joey said
no autographs.

333
00:13:39,402 --> 00:13:41,655
But if she's getting one,
I want one, too.

334
00:13:41,696 --> 00:13:44,532
"To Monica," and none of
this "best wishes" crap.

335
00:13:44,574 --> 00:13:46,159
I want "love."

336
00:13:47,744 --> 00:13:49,120
Actually, Mon,

337
00:13:49,162 --> 00:13:51,372
Matthew was giving me
his phone number.

338
00:13:51,414 --> 00:13:52,707
Oh, man...

339
00:13:52,749 --> 00:13:54,501
If I had known I was
coming to this party,

340
00:13:54,542 --> 00:13:56,377
I never would've
gotten married.

341
00:13:56,419 --> 00:13:58,588
It's nice to
meet you, Rachel.

342
00:13:58,630 --> 00:14:00,131
Nice to meet you.
Call me.

343
00:14:00,173 --> 00:14:01,675
We will.

344
00:14:03,384 --> 00:14:06,554
Look at you,
with all the guys.

345
00:14:06,596 --> 00:14:08,222
Guess you've forgotten
all about Joey.

346
00:14:08,264 --> 00:14:09,557
Yeah, well.

347
00:14:09,599 --> 00:14:11,392
I guess I have
forgotten about Joey.

348
00:14:11,434 --> 00:14:13,728
And clearly, you've
forgotten about Chandler.

349
00:14:13,770 --> 00:14:15,062
Oh, please.

350
00:14:15,104 --> 00:14:16,731
Chandler's
the love of my life.

351
00:14:16,773 --> 00:14:19,108
Whoa, leather pants.
Have mercy.

352
00:14:21,695 --> 00:14:24,238
So, uh... it's probably
going to be hard

353
00:14:24,280 --> 00:14:26,157
for you to leave
Boston, huh?

354
00:14:26,199 --> 00:14:27,867
Actually, I'm kind of
happy to be leaving.

355
00:14:27,908 --> 00:14:29,243
I just broke up
with someone.

356
00:14:29,285 --> 00:14:33,080
Oh, so sad.

357
00:14:33,122 --> 00:14:35,458
Still, it can't be easy
for you to leave Harvard.

358
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:37,293
Especially after
working alongside

359
00:14:37,335 --> 00:14:40,087
a Nobel prize winner
like Albert Wintermeyer.

360
00:14:40,129 --> 00:14:43,299
Actually, Albie's the
guy I broke up with.

361
00:14:43,341 --> 00:14:45,635
You dated Albert Wintermeyer?

362
00:14:45,677 --> 00:14:46,803
Yeah.

363
00:14:46,845 --> 00:14:50,557
And you called him "Albie"?

364
00:14:50,598 --> 00:14:54,853
I mean, that's like calling
Albert Einstein, uh...

365
00:14:54,894 --> 00:14:57,480
Albie.

366
00:14:57,522 --> 00:15:00,608
Yeah, well, he is
a brilliant man.

367
00:15:00,650 --> 00:15:02,318
Ya think?
I mean, you went out with

368
00:15:02,360 --> 00:15:04,696
the guy who
improved the accuracy
of radiocarbon dating

369
00:15:04,738 --> 00:15:07,323
by a factor of ten.
Yes.

370
00:15:07,365 --> 00:15:10,910
And while that is everything
one looks for in a boyfriend,

371
00:15:10,951 --> 00:15:12,871
he had a lot of issues.

372
00:15:12,911 --> 00:15:14,163
Oh, like what?

373
00:15:15,122 --> 00:15:16,999
I'm sorry,
I don't mean to pry.

374
00:15:17,041 --> 00:15:19,960
It's just that this must be
what regular people experience

375
00:15:20,002 --> 00:15:22,797
when they watch
Access Hollywood.

376
00:15:22,839 --> 00:15:24,841
Okay, you want the dirt?

377
00:15:24,883 --> 00:15:27,218
Albie was seriously insecure.

378
00:15:27,260 --> 00:15:30,680
I mean, he was really
intimidated by the guy
I dated before him.

379
00:15:30,722 --> 00:15:34,308
Who's intimidating to a guy
who won the Nobel prize?

380
00:15:34,350 --> 00:15:35,560
A guy who won two.

381
00:15:35,602 --> 00:15:38,187
Two?

382
00:15:38,229 --> 00:15:41,399
Don't tell me you dated
Benjamin Hobart?

383
00:15:41,441 --> 00:15:43,401
Yeah, for three years.

384
00:15:43,443 --> 00:15:45,027
Oh, my God.

385
00:15:45,069 --> 00:15:47,655
Have you ever been in
a relationship with someone

386
00:15:47,697 --> 00:15:50,241
who hasn't won
the Nobel prize?

387
00:15:50,283 --> 00:15:52,285
No.

388
00:15:53,953 --> 00:15:56,247
But there was my first
boyfriend Billy.

389
00:15:56,289 --> 00:15:58,750
Oh, yeah, no Nobel prizes
for him?

390
00:15:58,792 --> 00:16:00,334
No.

391
00:16:00,376 --> 00:16:02,211
But he did just win the
MacArthur Genius Grant.

392
00:16:02,253 --> 00:16:06,132
( snorts ):
What a loser.

393
00:16:06,173 --> 00:16:07,425
Some more wine?

394
00:16:12,847 --> 00:16:14,181
Hey, Ross.
Hey.

395
00:16:14,223 --> 00:16:15,600
How's it going
with Charlie?

396
00:16:15,642 --> 00:16:17,226
Oh, great.
After I finish my wine

397
00:16:17,268 --> 00:16:21,773
I'm going to blow
my average-sized brains out.

398
00:16:21,815 --> 00:16:23,190
What's the matter?

399
00:16:23,232 --> 00:16:27,987
She only dates geniuses
and Nobel prize winners.

400
00:16:28,028 --> 00:16:30,782
Oh, my God, at the Chinese
restaurant earlier today

401
00:16:30,824 --> 00:16:32,366
I put chopsticks in my mouth

402
00:16:32,408 --> 00:16:34,410
and pretended to be
a woolly mammoth.

403
00:16:34,452 --> 00:16:37,872
I always loved that.

404
00:16:37,914 --> 00:16:40,500
Of course you would, your brains
are smaller than mine.

405
00:16:42,418 --> 00:16:45,630
I can't compete with the guys
she goes out with.

406
00:16:45,672 --> 00:16:48,716
They're so out of my league.

407
00:16:48,758 --> 00:16:49,801
Oh, my God.

408
00:16:49,843 --> 00:16:50,969
Worse?

409
00:16:51,010 --> 00:16:52,553
Oh, much, much worse.

410
00:16:52,595 --> 00:16:54,472
I did my impression
of Joan Rivers

411
00:16:54,514 --> 00:16:56,474
as one of the earliest
amphibians.

412
00:16:56,516 --> 00:16:57,809
"Can we walk?"

413
00:16:57,851 --> 00:16:59,602
( giggles )

414
00:16:59,644 --> 00:17:01,395
Oh, you like that?

415
00:17:01,437 --> 00:17:02,981
No.
What?

416
00:17:03,063 --> 00:17:05,232
Come on.

417
00:17:05,274 --> 00:17:07,234
I think that's funny.

418
00:17:07,276 --> 00:17:08,862
Woolly mammoth.

419
00:17:08,903 --> 00:17:10,237
Come on.

420
00:17:10,279 --> 00:17:12,991
( applause )

421
00:17:13,031 --> 00:17:14,659
Well, I bet you're
all thinking

422
00:17:14,701 --> 00:17:16,327
that now would be
a really great time

423
00:17:16,369 --> 00:17:17,662
for an intermission, huh?

424
00:17:17,704 --> 00:17:20,915
( sighs ):
Oh, yes. God, yes.

425
00:17:20,957 --> 00:17:23,083
Well, you're not going
to get one.

426
00:17:23,125 --> 00:17:26,546
'Cause in life there are
no intermissions, people.

427
00:17:27,630 --> 00:17:29,340
Now, Chapter Seven:

428
00:17:29,382 --> 00:17:31,634
Divorce is a Four-Letter Word.

429
00:17:33,302 --> 00:17:35,972
How could he leave me?!

430
00:17:36,014 --> 00:17:39,600
I-I don't know, you seem lovely.

431
00:17:47,066 --> 00:17:48,609
Hey, Ross.

432
00:17:48,651 --> 00:17:49,944
This is one
of my co-stars, Dirk.

433
00:17:49,986 --> 00:17:51,445
Dirk, this is
my good friend Ross.

434
00:17:51,487 --> 00:17:52,697
Nice to meet you.
Hey.

435
00:17:52,739 --> 00:17:54,156
So what show you on?

436
00:17:54,198 --> 00:17:55,241
Oh, I'm not an actor.

437
00:17:55,282 --> 00:17:56,576
I'm a professor
of paleontology.

438
00:17:57,911 --> 00:17:59,370
That's science.

439
00:17:59,412 --> 00:18:01,288
Oh.

440
00:18:01,330 --> 00:18:03,249
Hey, well, listen,
I play a scientist on Days.

441
00:18:03,290 --> 00:18:05,543
My character just won
a Nobel prize.

442
00:18:07,169 --> 00:18:09,213
Hey, Ross, listen,

443
00:18:09,255 --> 00:18:11,549
Dirk was wondering about
the woman that you brought.

444
00:18:11,591 --> 00:18:13,426
And if you guys
are together or...?

445
00:18:13,467 --> 00:18:15,845
Oh, um, well, no, but...

446
00:18:15,887 --> 00:18:19,974
she only goes out with
really, really smart guys.

447
00:18:20,016 --> 00:18:23,561
Hey, I got a 690
on my SATs.

448
00:18:23,603 --> 00:18:26,606
I'd lead with that.

449
00:18:28,482 --> 00:18:32,152
That's it, just sign
right on the bra.

450
00:18:32,194 --> 00:18:33,529
Monica!

451
00:18:33,571 --> 00:18:37,324
Don't worry, Joe,
I won't come next year.

452
00:18:42,455 --> 00:18:43,957
What do you got there?

453
00:18:43,998 --> 00:18:46,626
Oh, just some boys
gave me their phone numbers.

454
00:18:46,667 --> 00:18:47,710
Really? Let me see.

455
00:18:47,752 --> 00:18:49,503
Oh.

456
00:18:49,545 --> 00:18:52,298
Damn, that's a lot of guys.

457
00:18:52,339 --> 00:18:53,883
Are you a little slutty?

458
00:18:53,925 --> 00:18:58,721
I think I am.

459
00:18:58,763 --> 00:19:01,307
Let me see if I approve
of any of these clowns.

460
00:19:01,348 --> 00:19:04,310
Oh, this guy wears a rug.

461
00:19:04,351 --> 00:19:07,063
This guy's Canadian.

462
00:19:07,105 --> 00:19:09,607
And this guy
is in a cult, okay?

463
00:19:09,649 --> 00:19:12,485
It'll cost you $5,000
to get to level three.

464
00:19:12,526 --> 00:19:14,070
And I don't feel
any different.

465
00:19:16,405 --> 00:19:18,491
Pass... oh, pass.
Pass.

466
00:19:18,532 --> 00:19:20,910
Why? Why? What's wrong
with these guys?

467
00:19:20,952 --> 00:19:22,120
Well, nothing major.

468
00:19:22,161 --> 00:19:23,579
It's just that, you know,

469
00:19:23,621 --> 00:19:25,581
they're not really
good enough for you.

470
00:19:25,623 --> 00:19:27,083
And you deserve the best.

471
00:19:27,125 --> 00:19:30,252
Joey, you're so sweet.

472
00:19:30,294 --> 00:19:31,754
It's true.

473
00:19:31,796 --> 00:19:33,255
But it doesn't matter
because I already know

474
00:19:33,297 --> 00:19:34,882
who you're going to
go home with tonight.

475
00:19:34,924 --> 00:19:36,300
Who?

476
00:19:39,595 --> 00:19:40,972
Me.

477
00:19:41,014 --> 00:19:43,641
What?

478
00:19:43,683 --> 00:19:45,143
Really?

479
00:19:45,184 --> 00:19:47,145
Yeah, because
we live together.

480
00:19:47,186 --> 00:19:48,312
It's a joke.

481
00:19:48,354 --> 00:19:49,355
Oh, I get it!

482
00:19:49,396 --> 00:19:51,732
Right, that's funny.
I get it.

483
00:19:51,774 --> 00:19:53,776
Got ya.
You're funny, Joey.

484
00:19:55,111 --> 00:19:56,946
( sighs )

485
00:20:03,703 --> 00:20:05,913
So, how did you enjoy
the play?

486
00:20:05,955 --> 00:20:08,041
Oh, my God, honey,

487
00:20:08,082 --> 00:20:11,335
I am so, so, so,
so, so, so sorry.

488
00:20:11,377 --> 00:20:12,670
Well, you should be.

489
00:20:12,712 --> 00:20:14,338
You missed the most
powerful three hours

490
00:20:14,380 --> 00:20:16,174
in the history
of the theater.

491
00:20:16,215 --> 00:20:18,592
You really liked it?
Oh, yeah.

492
00:20:18,634 --> 00:20:20,845
I mean, at first I hated it.
But why wouldn't I?

493
00:20:20,887 --> 00:20:24,598
Because as a man, I've been
trained not to listen!

494
00:20:26,934 --> 00:20:29,311
But after Chapter 16:

495
00:20:29,353 --> 00:20:33,357
Fat, Single
and Ready to Mingle,

496
00:20:33,399 --> 00:20:36,318
I was uplifted.

497
00:20:36,360 --> 00:20:37,695
Wow, really?

498
00:20:37,737 --> 00:20:39,989
Oh, yeah, I had no idea
of the amazing journey

499
00:20:40,031 --> 00:20:41,365
you go through as a woman.

500
00:20:41,407 --> 00:20:45,536
Tell me... tell me about
your first period.

501
00:20:45,578 --> 00:20:48,539
No.

502
00:20:49,957 --> 00:20:51,876
Did somebody sign your bra?

503
00:20:51,918 --> 00:20:53,669
So I got it
when I was 13.

504
00:20:53,711 --> 00:20:56,214
Very difficult and it...

505
00:20:56,254 --> 00:20:58,298
Hey, Ross.

506
00:20:58,340 --> 00:21:01,886
So, listen, about you
and the dinosaur girl...

507
00:21:01,928 --> 00:21:03,387
Are you really
just going to let

508
00:21:03,429 --> 00:21:05,723
a couple of Nobel prizes
scare you off?

509
00:21:05,765 --> 00:21:08,226
What is that?
Come on, a piece of paper?

510
00:21:08,267 --> 00:21:10,477
It's actually a
million dollar prize.

511
00:21:10,519 --> 00:21:12,855
Go, Charlie.

512
00:21:12,897 --> 00:21:15,608
But my point is...

513
00:21:15,649 --> 00:21:17,235
okay, so she dated them,

514
00:21:17,275 --> 00:21:19,195
but she also
broke up with them.

515
00:21:19,237 --> 00:21:21,781
Maybe she's looking
to, you know,

516
00:21:21,822 --> 00:21:25,576
slum it with
some average Joe PhD.

517
00:21:25,618 --> 00:21:28,495
Yeah, maybe.

518
00:21:28,537 --> 00:21:31,749
And I do have my whole
career in front of me.

519
00:21:31,791 --> 00:21:36,170
I mean, I-I could still
win a Nobel prize.

520
00:21:36,212 --> 00:21:39,506
Although, the last two
papers I've written

521
00:21:39,548 --> 00:21:43,219
were widely discredited.

522
00:21:43,261 --> 00:21:46,097
You're so much more
than just brains.

523
00:21:46,139 --> 00:21:49,142
You are sweet
and kind and funny.

524
00:21:49,183 --> 00:21:50,517
And sexy.

525
00:21:50,559 --> 00:21:54,939
Okay, well, give her
a chance to see all that.

526
00:21:54,981 --> 00:21:56,149
Yeah. Yeah.

527
00:21:56,190 --> 00:21:57,191
You're right.
Thanks, Pheebs.

528
00:21:57,233 --> 00:21:58,276
I'm going to go find her.

529
00:21:58,317 --> 00:22:00,027
Good for you.

530
00:22:00,069 --> 00:22:02,529
Hey, and I thought your paper
on punctuated equilibrium

531
00:22:02,571 --> 00:22:04,240
in the Devonian Era
was top notch.

532
00:22:04,282 --> 00:22:06,492
Stop going
through my stuff.

533
00:22:09,495 --> 00:22:10,830
Hey.
Hey.

534
00:22:10,871 --> 00:22:12,248
I just wanted
to let you know,

535
00:22:12,290 --> 00:22:13,582
I've changed my mind.

536
00:22:13,624 --> 00:22:15,626
I'm going to do it,
I'm going to kiss Joey.

537
00:22:15,668 --> 00:22:17,628
No. You can't.

538
00:22:17,670 --> 00:22:19,630
Friends hooking up
is a bad idea.

539
00:22:19,672 --> 00:22:21,799
Please, what about you
and Chandler?

540
00:22:21,841 --> 00:22:24,927
That's different.
I was drunk and stupid.

541
00:22:24,969 --> 00:22:28,222
Well, hello!

542
00:22:28,264 --> 00:22:29,556
What about all the guys

543
00:22:29,598 --> 00:22:31,600
that you got
the phone numbers from?

544
00:22:31,642 --> 00:22:33,393
Why don't you just
kiss one of them?

545
00:22:33,435 --> 00:22:35,562
I could, I could,
but I don't want to.

546
00:22:35,604 --> 00:22:38,482
I want to kiss Joey.

547
00:22:38,524 --> 00:22:40,151
All right.

548
00:22:40,193 --> 00:22:42,987
I think it's a big mistake,
but it's your decision.

549
00:22:47,449 --> 00:22:48,826
I'm going to do it.

550
00:22:48,868 --> 00:22:50,244
And I can't stop you.

551
00:22:50,286 --> 00:22:51,369
No.

552
00:23:04,258 --> 00:23:05,592
Hey, Rach,

553
00:23:05,634 --> 00:23:07,385
have you seen
Charlie anywhere?

554
00:23:13,893 --> 00:23:16,520
I'm smarter than him!

555
00:23:23,443 --> 00:23:25,905
Thank you so much for
these tickets, Chandler.

556
00:23:25,946 --> 00:23:28,157
This was a really important
experience for me

557
00:23:28,199 --> 00:23:30,201
and I wanted
to share it with you.

558
00:23:30,243 --> 00:23:32,328
You're so wonderful.

559
00:23:33,287 --> 00:23:36,957
Why don't you like me?

560
00:23:36,999 --> 00:23:41,879
Chapter One: My First Period.

561
00:23:42,213 --> 00:23:43,505
Can't believe you guys
bought that.

562
00:23:43,547 --> 00:23:44,590
Enjoy your slow death.

563
00:23:46,259 --> 00:23:47,425
